I just read David Hairston's well-thought-out article on the subject of
confirmations in nethack.  It brought back a very special feeling that
many of us undoubtedly share.

Nethack is a whole world, exciting and intriguing, humorous and whimsical,
adhering to its own bizarre logic.  The way I like to think of it, Nethack
is a world, and the program is the interface to that world (much the same
way one might regard Usenet as a world, and 'nn' as an interface).

With that in mind, when discussing nethack we must consider two things:
how should the world of nethack behave, and how should the interface
function.  For instance, how a pet should respond when attacked applies
to the world, whereas the question of confirming an attack on a pet
applies more to the interface.

With THAT in mind, an important META-question seems to be: how powerful,
flexible, idiot-proof, line-noise-proof, etc. should the interface be?

That's not an easy question to answer.  In real life, if I sense a
dangerous situation, I will avoid it.  Most often, it is a conscious
thought: I think I'd better not try to cross the street yet.  Sometimes,
however, the defense it automatic: Wow! I don't know how my foot got
to the brake pedal, but thank Loki it got there in time!

So, everyone has a unique survival algorithm in real life.  I would suggest,
therefore, that it is reasonable to allow players to choose their own
'intrinsic survival instincts' within the realm of nethack.  After all,
I can decide that, from now on, I will always check my shoes for scorpions
before putting them on, and really do the checking, and be the safer for it
(I really do this :-).

However, I cannot suddenly decide that from now on, I will constantly be on
the lookout for imminent death by: falling satellites, angry zebras,
poorly aimed fireworks, etc... and then state with confidence that I will
never die any of those ways.  It is just plain impossible for one person
to effectively monitor all those things and get anywhere.

Perhaps a reasonable situation would be to allow each player a limited
number of 'intrinsic survival instincts' per game.

Or maybe not.  Anyway, the main thrust of this article was perhaps to
offer some perspective, and to voice a special reverence for the bizarre
world of nethack.

